(a) The administration of this article is vested in the city code enforcement officer and police chief, except as otherwise specifically provided, as follows:
(1) The police chief shall make police department facilities and personnel available to fingerprint and photograph an applicant for a license. The cost of fingerprinting and photographing shall be paid by the applicant at the time the service is provided. Records of fingerprints and photographs so obtained shall be maintained at the police department in the normal course of business.
(2) The police chief shall have a criminal history inquiry conducted on all persons listed as applicants in a license application. The inquiry shall be limited to determining whether an applicant for a license has a criminal conviction of the type described and for the period indicated in section 10-276. The police chief shall notify the mayor in the event such criminal conviction is found to exist.
(3) The city code enforcement officer shall notify the mayor whether an applicant or designated license holder in a license application has failed to comply with or is in violation of applicable provisions of the zoning ordinances, or the building codes, development standards or other land use ordinances and regulations of the city relating to the business to be operated under the license.
(4) Nothing herein is intended to prohibit or restrict the police chief or the city code enforcement officer from providing additional assistance as may derive from their normal duties and responsibilities or as may be requested by the mayor. In no event shall any assistance as described herein or the absence of such assistance, delay or extend the time period specified in section 10-278 for the mayor to act on a license application.
(b) An application for a license, or for the renewal of a license, made pursuant to this article shall be submitted to the mayor, who shall grant or deny the application in accordance with the provisions of this article. The mayor shall also suspend or revoke a license in accordance with the provisions of this article.
(c) A license issued pursuant to this article shall expire on December 31 of the year in and for which it is issued and may be renewed annually.
(d) A license, if granted, shall state on its face:
(1) The name of the license holder;
(2) The name and address of the licensed premises;
(3) The date of issuance of the license;
(4) The particular classification or classifications of sexually oriented business for which the license is granted; and
(5) The license number.
(e) The license of the sexually oriented business shall be classified as follows, based upon the information in the application and subject to subsequent inspection for verification:
(1) Adult arcade;
(2) Adult bookstore;
(3) Adult cabaret;
(4) Adult encounter parlor;
(5) Adult modeling studio;
(6) Adult movie theater;
(7) Adult video facility;
(8) Bathhouse;
(9) Escort agency; or
(10) Massage parlor.
(Ord. No. 09-03, § 1(10-253), 10-14-2009)

375:
A private cemetery will be authorized and a permit therefor issued, pursuant to application, in accordance with the following requirements:
(1) A plat of survey prepared by a registered land surveyor licensed in the state shall be prepared and filed with the city clerk along with the application;
(2) A cemetery survey shall indicate that the cemetery contains not less than two acres;
(3) The location of the proposed cemetery is compatible with adjacent land uses, existing or proposed highways and any other elements or factors deemed by the city to affect the public health, safety and welfare of the inhabitants of the area surrounding the proposed location; the proposed site must contain a buffered area having a width of not less than ten feet which must be suitably landscaped and maintained with trees and shrubs; and
(4) Adequate access is available by public street or private or restricted means of ingress and egress.
(Ord. of 9-8-2014, § 1(3-31))

403:
The following conditions are declared to be nuisances and shall be abated in accordance with this article:
(1) Stagnant water on any lot, whether vacant or built upon;
(2) The pollution of public waters, or the injection of matter into the sewer system which would be damaging to the system;
(3) The generation of smoke or fumes in sufficient amounts to cause odor or general annoyance to the public;
(4) Obstruction of a public street, highway or sidewalk without a permit;
(5) Any trees, shrubbery or other plants or parts which obstruct clear, safe vision on streets and intersections within the city;
(6) Any discarded refrigerators, stoves or other appliances;
(7) Any abandoned vehicles;
(8) Any dead or decaying matter upon any lot which is odorous or generally annoying to the public, or which may cause disease;
(9) Maintaining a dangerous or diseased animal;
(10) Overgrown lots, whether vacant or built upon;
(11) Vacant structures which have become deteriorated through neglect, nonuse, fire, vandalism or the elements;
(12) Any condition not declared a nuisance by this article, but declared to be such elsewhere in this Code; and
(13) Any other condition constituting a nuisance under state law.
(Code 2002, § 12-52; Ord. of 5-14-2001(3), § 16-5-3)

